sentences.t:
Sonando van sus nuevas, alent parte del mar [andon];
alegre era el Çid e todas sus compañas,
que Dios le ayudara e fiziera esta arrancada.

sentences.l:
Sounding go his tidings, beyond part of the sea [they-go];
glad was the Çid and all his companies,
that God him had-helped and had-made this rout.

sentences.t:
Davan sus corredores e fazien las trasnochadas,
llegan a Gujera e llegan a Xátiva,
aun mas ayusso, a Denia la casa;

sentences.l:
They-were-sending his raiders and they-were-making the night-marches,
they-reach to Gujera and they-reach to Xátiva,
even more down, to Denia the town;

sentences.t:
Sópolo mio Çid, de coraçón le plaz;
salió de Murviedro una noch [a] trasnocha[r]
amaneció a mio Çid en tierras de Mon Real.

sentences.l:
Learned-it my Çid, from heart to-him it-pleases;
he-went-out of Murviedro one night [to] night-march
dawned to my Çid in lands of Mon Real.

sentences.t:
Quien quiere perder cueta e venir a rritad,
viniesse a mio Çid que a sabor de cavalgar;
çercar quiere a Valençia pora cristianos la dar:

sentences.l:
Who wants to-lose affliction and come to wealth,
let-him-come to my Çid who has desire of riding;
to-besiege wants Valençia for Christians it to-give:

sentences.t:
Los que f[o]ron de pie cavalleros se fazen;
el oro e la plata ¿quien vos lo podrie contar?
Todos eran ricos quantos que allí ha.

sentences.l:
Those who were of foot horsemen themselves make;
the gold and the silver, who to-you it could count?
All were rich as-many-as there are.
